ng to  encourage consumers to go out and get  vaccinated when they could get access to  the vaccine and that was a mistake we  sort of  seeded the information marketplace to  people who were putting out a counter  message  one of the things that we began to  notice when we really began to look at  vaccination data in the u.s in detail  was that there was this  significant split emerging and so you  can see on this county level map here  where red and orange places have really  low rates of vaccination  if you take a look at these places in  orange and red like the mountain west  down here in louisiana arkansas and  mississippi places in florida and  michigan a lot of those places are the  same places that have been very very  hard hit by the delta variant  levels of vaccine hesitancy remain  relatively high in the us  but the phenomenon starts to show up in  other countries as well  an example of that globally  is with china and russia  china and russia very early on made the  decision that they were going to start  administering their vaccines well before  they had been fully tested  the lasting effect of that decision  is that it really shook everyone's  confidence in those vaccines both the  people in china and russia and also the  people who were contracted to buy their  vaccines  you can see the results particularly in  russia a lot of the russian people are  still very suspicious of the russian  sputnik vaccine  in particular  would be  in some ways it's unfortunate because  overall there are safe and effective  vaccines uh just like a lot of the  western vaccines but these are the kinds  of decisions that public health  officials have to make in a tough  situation and sometimes there's no right  answer  meanwhile another crisis is brewing the  rise of a devastating new variant  delta really reared its head in india  around march april 2021 that was when we  saw the numbers increasing everyone  thought we'd  but beyond the worst of the coronavirus  what officials in india call a tsunami  of coronavirus cases nearly one million  in the last three days alone  now the indian healthcare system on the  best of days is inadequate when delta  started flaring up in india what we saw  is that hospitals were so overflowing  that even the richest couldn't access  healthcare we don't have anything  available  and patients are dying hospitals were  running out of basic supplies like  oxygen  we saw crematoriums overflowing  it was a desperate situation in india  with delta ripping through its  population india is forced to make a  decision with global consequences  the syrian institute of india ended up  having to  re-direct much of its supply to its own  domestic needs  we saw india discontinue exports to  kovacs and that really meant that  african countries at that time did not  have anywhere else to turn  midway through the year kovacs has only  distributed 88 million doses far short  of its original goal of 1.8 billion by  the end of the year  we did an analysis of the gap between  wealthy countries and the poorest  countries and their access to these  vaccines and we found that  the wealthiest countries were getting  vaccinated at a rate that was uh 25  times faster than the poorest countries  in the poorest countries they're still  struggling to to get basic levels of  vaccination  and the countries are suffering for it  every time vaccines came supplies would  be over in just a matter of hours  i remember when i tried to go get my  first shot for the estradica vaccine  i waited a whole day in in a queue and i  could not get it so i only managed to  get my first shot after queueing for  days  it really showed  how big the divide is  the vaccine campaign in the united  states kind of hit its low point in  july 2021  and that is largely because things were  going really well at that point it was  summertime we celebrated july 4th as  kind of like a turning point  but very swiftly after that  came the delta mutation  the delta mutation the vaccine is less  effective against it  it still keeps people out of the  hospitals and prevents a lot of deaths  but people started getting and spreading  and transmitting the virus more and more  the delta variant in fact already  sending unvaccinated americans to the  hospital in alarming numbers our  inpatient units are at capacity  and so  people have to  either get better enough to go home  or if they succumb to their illnesses  and die that opens up a bed  the delta variant because it is so  contagious does a unfortunately a very  good job finding people who haven't yet  been vaccinated  nurses are not only tired but in short  supply  we have a very low vaccination rate in  our community here in idaho  and nobody's masked  and people are like yeah  it's all open it's all free and it's  devastating to me will the vaccine save  them now  no  but you could have done that before  soon after delta's arrival in the u.s  evidence starts to emerge that the  vaccine's efficacy is waning over time  the problem is first identified in  israel one of the world's leaders in  vaccine administration and data tracking  you had israel collecting more  systematic data and they were the first  to pick that up cdc actually dismissed  the israeli data when it first emerged  in the summer  cdc is probably going to do a really  good analysis of that and tell us  exactly how many breakthrough infections  occurred  six months later but it doesn't help  inform decisions that have to get made  in real time like should we be  administering boosters to high risk  populations  the news is not as bleak in other parts  of the world  india's infection rate having peaked in  may continues to plummet as the  country's vaccination program finally  gathers steam vaccinations increased  sevenfold from april to august  [Music]  in brazil whose anemic early campaign  costs hundreds of thousands of lives a  stunning turnaround is underway  in june july you really started uh  seeing more of a